reply.body:
This could be a valid mapping if `subobjects: false` is set on the mapping root. Additionally, not handling this case leads to inconsistent behavior depending on the order in which the values are inserted:

```java
{
  // ok
  Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
  XContentMapValues.insertValue("x.y.z", map, "value3");
  XContentMapValues.insertValue("x.y", map, "value1");
  XContentMapValues.insertValue("x", map, "value2");
}

{
  // not ok
  Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
  XContentMapValues.insertValue("x.y", map, "value1");
  XContentMapValues.insertValue("x", map, "value2");
  XContentMapValues.insertValue("x.y.z", map, "value3");  <--- ERROR
}
```

This is a bad code smell to me and a fairly clear indicator we should handle this case. The downstream mapper validation can determine if the resulting map is actually a valid document, dependent on what subobjects are (or are not) allowed.

